        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">02 <span>//</span> The belt</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h">A thin band around the earth</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Coffee grows between the <strong>Tropic of Cancer</strong> and the <strong>Tropic of Capricorn</strong> (~23.5°N–23.5°S). Specialty Peru is overwhelmingly <strong>arabica</strong> on the eastern Andean slopes — the ceja de selva.</p>
                    <ol class="talk-steps">
                        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Two lines mark the tropics</strong><p>Arabica prefers cooler highland tropics. Robusta tolerates hotter lowlands.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Peru on the east face</strong><p>Eastern slopes, cloud-forest transition. Typical arabica suitability ~800–2,200 m.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Volume leaders are not the specialty map</strong><p>National green coffee, USDA: Cajamarca ~22% · San Martín ~20% · Junín ~19% · Amazonas ~15%.</p></div></li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-belt">
                    <div class="talk-map" data-talk-map="belt" aria-label="World map, each coffee country in its own color"></div>
                    <p class="talk-belt__cap">Colors = origin countries, not a continuous shaded strip. Peru in purple. World leaders by green-bean volume sit in this band: Brazil, Vietnam, Colombia, Indonesia, Ethiopia.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">03 · The country</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h">Peru, and its valleys</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">These are the official 2023 department limits, drawn as vectors. Coffee is not on the coast and not on the high plateau. It is on the eastern slope, where the Andes drop into the Amazon. The next maps open those valleys.</p>
                    <ul class="talk-legend">
                        <li><i style="background:#E07A3D"></i> Cajamarca</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#2F6B4F"></i> Amazonas</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#7CB342"></i> San Martín</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#E6B325"></i> Piura</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#1AA6A6"></i> Huánuco</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#3D8BFF"></i> Pasco</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#5B4BDB"></i> Junín</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#C45C26"></i> Ayacucho</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#722EA8"></i> Cusco</li>
                        <li><i style="background:#C23B6E"></i> Puno</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-map" data-talk-map="country" aria-label="Full outline of Peru with coffee departments filled"></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split talk-split--chart">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">04 · The climb</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h">From the sea upward</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Four real cuts, west to east. Cusco is the front ridge. Coffee is the drop after the crest, not the summit.</p>
                    <ol class="talk-steps">
                        <li><span>N</span><div><strong>North</strong><p>Lower crests. Jaén and San Ignacio sit on this face, 900–1,950 m.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>H</span><div><strong>Huánuco</strong><p>The town of Tingo María is jungle. The farms are up the slope, to 2,000 m.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>J</span><div><strong>Junín</strong><p>Chanchamayo and Villa Rica. A day’s drive from Lima. 900–1,800 m.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>C</span><div><strong>Cusco</strong><p>La Convención, 900–2,000 m. The white line is this cut.</p></div></li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <img class="talk-cut" src="~/brand/peru-cuts.svg" asp-append-version="true" alt="West-to-east elevation cuts. Cusco is the front layer. Coffee grows on the eastern drop." />
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">05 <span>//</span> Altitude → density → flavor</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h talk-h--serif">Why altitude</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Higher elevation, cooler means and a diurnal swing, then <strong>slower cherry maturation</strong>, a denser seed, more sugars and acids. Elevation is a <strong>proxy</strong>, not destiny.</p>
                    <table class="talk-mech">
                        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Cup</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Cool / high</td><td>Slower ripening</td><td>Time for sugars and acids</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Diurnal swing</td><td>Seed allocation</td><td>Denser cellular structure</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Density</td><td>Different roast heat uptake</td><td>Bright, complex when roasted well</td></tr>
                            <tr class="is-caveat"><td>Caveat</td><td>Moisture, variety, process, latitude</td><td>Density is not automatic “better”</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="talk-line"><b>Talk line</b> Higher usually means denser and more acid-driven complexity — if the cherry was ripe, the variety can express, and processing didn’t erase it.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-elev">
                    <svg class="talk-elev__svg" viewBox="-10 -10 740 440" role="img" aria-label="West to east section. The lime band is typical arabica, about 800 to 2200 meters.">
                        <line x1="90" y1="40" x2="90" y2="340" stroke="rgba(26,25,23,0.12)" stroke-width="1"/>
                        <line x1="250" y1="40" x2="250" y2="340" stroke="rgba(26,25,23,0.12)" stroke-width="1"/>
                        <line x1="420" y1="40" x2="420" y2="340" stroke="rgba(26,25,23,0.18)" stroke-width="1"/>
                        <line x1="560" y1="40" x2="560" y2="340" stroke="rgba(26,25,23,0.12)" stroke-width="1"/>
                        <rect x="40" y="300" width="50" height="40" fill="none" stroke="#1A1917" stroke-width="1"/>
                        <line x1="45" y1="310" x2="85" y2="310" stroke="#1A1917" stroke-width="0.6"/>
                        <line x1="45" y1="318" x2="85" y2="318" stroke="#1A1917" stroke-width="0.6"/>
                        <line x1="45" y1="326" x2="85" y2="326" stroke="#1A1917" stroke-width="0.6"/>
                        <path d="M90,300 L140,295 L180,280 L220,230 L250,160 L280,90 L310,70 L340,85 L380,130 L420,190 L460,240 L500,265 L560,280 L650,290 L680,292" fill="none" stroke="#1A1917" stroke-width="1.6"/>
                        <path d="M250,160 L280,90 L310,70 L340,85 L380,130 L420,190 L250,160 Z" fill="#1A1917" fill-opacity="0.08"/>
                        <rect x="400" y="170" width="40" height="130" fill="#D4FF00" fill-opacity="0.55" stroke="#1A1917" stroke-width="1"/>
                        <text x="700" y="300" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="8" fill="#6E6A64">0 M</text>
                        <text x="700" y="230" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="8" fill="#6E6A64">1000</text>
                        <text x="700" y="160" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="8" fill="#6E6A64">2000</text>
                        <text x="700" y="90" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="8" fill="#6E6A64">4000+</text>
                        <text transform="translate(106,384) rotate(-90)" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="10" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1A1917">COAST</text>
                        <text transform="translate(258,335) rotate(-90)" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="10" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1A1917">ANDES</text>
                        <text transform="translate(454,335) rotate(-90)" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="10" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1A1917">CEJA DE SELVA</text>
                        <text transform="translate(568,335) rotate(-90)" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="10" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1A1917">AMAZON</text>
                        <text x="40" y="410" font-family="JetBrains Mono,monospace" font-size="9" letter-spacing="1.5" fill="#8A847A">W → E SECTION · LIME = TYPICAL ARABICA BAND ~800–2200 M</text>
                    </svg>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide talk-zones">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">06 <span>//</span> Ten-minute map</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h talk-zones__title">Peru in 7 zones</h2>
                    <ol class="talk-steps talk-steps--tight">
                        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Far north — Piura</strong><p>Huancabamba valleys. Small volume. ~900–2,000 m.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>North highlands — Cajamarca</strong><p>San Ignacio, Jaén. ~22% of national production.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Amazonas</strong><p>Rodríguez de Mendoza. ~15% of national production.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>04</span><div><strong>San Martín</strong><p>Moyobamba / Alto Mayo. ~20%. Often the lower belt.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Central jungle</strong><p>Chanchamayo and Villa Rica DO. Junín ~19%. Leoncio Prado is Huánuco.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>06</span><div><strong>Cusco / La Convención</strong><p>Inkawasi, Santa Teresa, Santa Ana. DO Machu Picchu–Huadquiña.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>07</span><div><strong>Puno / Sandia</strong><p>Southern high belt. Remote.</p></div></li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-zones__fig">
                    <img class="talk-relief" src="~/brand/peru-zones-relief.png" asp-append-version="true" alt="Peru in relief with the seven coffee zones marked." />
                    <svg class="talk-zones__pins" viewBox="-150 0 980 820" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
                        <g fill="none" stroke="#1A1917" stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.7">
                            <line x1="66" y1="219" x2="-8" y2="200"/>
                            <line x1="138" y1="281" x2="-8" y2="290"/>
                            <line x1="173" y1="232" x2="690" y2="175"/>
                            <line x1="239" y1="313" x2="690" y2="310"/>
                            <line x1="310" y1="488" x2="-8" y2="490"/>
                            <line x1="434" y1="554" x2="690" y2="520"/>
                            <line x1="612" y1="601" x2="690" y2="680"/>
                        </g>
                        <g fill="#FF6464">
                            <circle cx="66" cy="219" r="3.2"/>
                            <circle cx="138" cy="281" r="3.2"/>
                            <circle cx="173" cy="232" r="3.2"/>
                            <circle cx="239" cy="313" r="3.2"/>
                            <circle cx="310" cy="488" r="3.2"/>
                            <circle cx="434" cy="554" r="3.2"/>
                            <circle cx="612" cy="601" r="3.2"/>
                        </g>
                        <g font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="15" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="1.2" fill="#1A1917">
                            <text x="-145" y="196">PIURA</text>
                            <text x="-145" y="294">CAJAMARCA</text>
                            <text x="700" y="170">AMAZONAS</text>
                            <text x="700" y="306">SAN MARTÍN</text>
                            <text x="-145" y="494">CENTRAL</text>
                            <text x="700" y="512">CUSCO</text>
                            <text x="700" y="530" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#6E6A64">INKAWASI</text>
                            <text x="700" y="684">PUNO / SANDIA</text>
                        </g>
                    </svg>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">07 <span>//</span> North</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h">North</h2>
                    <ol class="talk-steps talk-steps--tight">
                        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Piura — Huancabamba</strong><p>Small volume. Semi-arid valleys. Typica, Caturra, Catimor in the trade notes. ~900–2,000 m.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Cajamarca — San Ignacio, Jaén</strong><p>Largest share of Peru’s green coffee, about 22%. Nearly all of Cajamarca’s coffee is Jaén and San Ignacio. Heavy Cup of Excellence history. ~900–1,950 m.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Amazonas — Rodríguez de Mendoza</strong><p>The specialty story is here. Bagua and Utcubamba are the volume neighbors. About 15%.</p></div></li>
                    </ol>
                    <p class="talk-stat is-north">La Catarata, outside Jaén, is a Cup of Excellence farm we visit</p>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-map" data-talk-map="north" aria-label="Jaén, San Ignacio, and Amazonas"></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">08 · Nearer Lima</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h is-center">Chanchamayo, Satipo, Villa Rica</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">This is the coffee most of the country grew up drinking. Junín’s Chanchamayo and Satipo, and Pasco’s Oxapampa — Villa Rica is a district inside Oxapampa. Published band: <strong>900–1,800 m</strong>. Junín is about <strong>19%</strong> of Peru’s green coffee.</p>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Lower and warmer than the high Cusco slopes. Chocolate, caramel, yellow and black fruit. <strong>Café Villa Rica</strong> is a protected denomination (Indecopi, 2010).</p>
                    <p class="talk-stat is-center">About a day’s drive from Lima</p>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-map" data-talk-map="central" aria-label="Chanchamayo, Satipo, and Oxapampa"></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">09 · Huánuco</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h is-center">Tingo María</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Almost all of Huánuco’s coffee is in <strong>Leoncio Prado</strong>, the province around Tingo María. Published band: <strong>900–2,000 m</strong>. The town itself is low jungle. If someone quotes the town’s elevation, they are not quoting the farm.</p>
                    <p class="talk-copy talk-copy--quiet">Useful teaching point: ask where on the slope the cherry was picked. The valley floor and the ridge are different coffees.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-map" data-talk-map="huanuco" aria-label="Leoncio Prado province around Tingo María"></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">10 · Cusco</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h is-south">La Convención</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Nearly all of Cusco’s coffee is this one province, the pale shape in the north of the department. Published band: <strong>900–2,000 m</strong>. Quillabamba (Santa Ana) is the valley town. Darker districts: <strong>Inkawasi</strong>, <strong>Santa Teresa</strong>, <strong>Echarate</strong>.</p>
                    <p class="talk-copy">This corridor has its own denomination: <strong>Café Machu Picchu–Huadquiña</strong> (Indecopi, 2011). Washed, honey, natural — the farms are close enough to taste side by side. This is our home map.</p>
                    <p class="talk-stat is-south">Inkawasi is Cusco, not Puno</p>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-map" data-talk-map="convencion" aria-label="La Convención province in Cusco"></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">11 · Puno</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h is-south">Sandia, not the lake</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Puno is famous for Lake Titicaca, about 3,800 m — too high and too cold for coffee. The cherry is in <strong>Sandia</strong>, the tropical valley that falls off the Andes toward Bolivia. Published band: <strong>900–1,800 m</strong>.</p>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Districts people name: San Juan del Oro, San Pedro de Putina Punco, Alto Inambari, Yanahuaya. CECOVASA, the Sandia co-op, zoned their own cups and found the <strong>1,500–1,800 m</strong> floor scored highest. Remote. When a Puno lot is good, it is because of that slope, not the altiplano.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-map" data-talk-map="sandia" aria-label="Sandia province in Puno"></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">12 · The country, in height</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h">Read the slope, not the pin</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">This picture is real elevation. Pale is low: the coast, and the Amazon. Violet is the Andes. The darkest violet is the crest.</p>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Coffee is the eastern face of that spine — the drop toward the jungle. Not the dry coast. Not the frozen top. The same drop the cuts earlier in the talk were measuring.</p>
                    <p class="talk-copy">A lot named for a town is still a place on that face. The town can sit in the valley while the farm is a few hundred meters above it. Ask for the meters.</p>
                </div>
                <img class="talk-relief" src="~/brand/peru-relief.png" asp-append-version="true" alt="Shaded relief of Peru. Pale land is low. The violet spine is the Andes. Coffee grows on the eastern drop." />
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <p class="talk-kicker">13 · Volume, not the specialty map</p>
            <h2 class="talk-h">Piura and San Martín</h2>
            <p class="talk-copy">Two zones sit beside the valleys we just opened. They move a lot of coffee. They are not the high-cup story.</p>
            <div class="talk-board">
                <article>
                    <p>01</p>
                    <h3>Piura — Huancabamba</h3>
                    <span>Far north. Small volume. Semi-arid valleys, about 900–2,000 m. Typica, Caturra, and Catimor show up in the trade notes.</span>
                </article>
                <article>
                    <p>02</p>
                    <h3>San Martín — ~20%</h3>
                    <span>Moyobamba, Rioja, Alto Mayo. Often the lower belt, about 900–1,200 m. Volume, not the cup that wins a table.</span>
                </article>
                <article>
                    <p>03</p>
                    <h3>National shares</h3>
                    <span>Cajamarca ~22% · San Martín ~20% · Junín ~19% · Amazonas ~15%. USDA green coffee. These are volume shares, not quality scores.</span>
                </article>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <p class="talk-kicker">14 · Varieties</p>
            <h2 class="talk-h">What the lot is actually called</h2>
            <p class="talk-copy">The name on the bag is a claim. Ask what was planted, and what was picked.</p>
            <div class="talk-board">
                <article>
                    <p>Typica</p>
                    <h3>Heritage</h3>
                    <span>Still prized when the cup pays. Most volume plots moved to Caturra and Catimor for yield and rust resistance.</span>
                </article>
                <article>
                    <p>Bourbon</p>
                    <h3>Red is the default</h3>
                    <span>Yellow is a mutation of red. Orange and pink need the lot checked. Pink is often not Bourbon at all.</span>
                </article>
                <article>
                    <p>Geisha</p>
                    <h3>Real, and also mislabeled</h3>
                    <span>Real plantings exist in Peru. A Geisha name on a bag is not proof of the variety in the cup.</span>
                </article>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split talk-split--names">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">15 · Names on the bag</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h">Special names</h2>
                    <ol class="talk-steps talk-steps--tight">
                        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Marsellesa</strong><p>A Sarchimor. Rust resistance and yield. Good at altitude, not exceptional. It is not Catimor CR-95.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>SL-09 / “Inca Geisha”</strong><p>Inkawasi samples have tested closer to SL-09 than to Geisha. The cup can be floral. Don’t tell it as settled history.</p></div></li>
                        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Orange Bourbon · Santa Teresa</strong><p>Bourbon is grown there. The color name travels loosely. Verify the lot before you say it.</p></div></li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="talk-names">
                    <img class="talk-names__map" src="~/brand/ethiopia-peru.svg" asp-append-version="true" alt="Ethiopia, home of Geisha, and Peru, where the Inkawasi lot is grown." />
                    <div class="talk-names__pair">
                        <div class="talk-names__card">
                            <b>Geisha</b>
                            <span>Core Ethiopia</span>
                        </div>
                        <i>≠ DNA</i>
                        <div class="talk-names__card is-legacy">
                            <b>SL-09</b>
                            <span>Ethiopian legacy</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="talk-names__note"><b>Inkawasi “Inca Geisha”</b> Cup can be Geisha-like. Genetics discussed for these samples: SL-09, not Geisha.</p>
                    <p class="talk-names__warn">Marsellesa ≠ Catimor CR-95. Rust-resistant names are not interchangeable.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <div class="talk-split talk-split--wheel">
                <div>
                    <p class="talk-kicker">16 · Flavor</p>
                    <h2 class="talk-h">Read the cup from the center out</h2>
                    <p class="talk-copy">Start at the center and move out. Washed is still most Peruvian specialty. Naturals and honeys are the accent. Two denominations: Café Villa Rica, and Café Machu Picchu–Huadquiña.</p>
                    <ol class="talk-steps">
                        <li class="is-center"><strong>Center</strong> — chocolate, caramel, a rounder cup. Chanchamayo, Villa Rica.</li>
                        <li class="is-north"><strong>North</strong> — brighter, sweeter, red and yellow fruit. San Ignacio.</li>
                        <li class="is-south"><strong>South</strong> — the process shows. Florals and fruit in La Convención and Sandia.</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <img class="talk-wheel" src="~/brand/coffee-wheel.svg" alt="SCA coffee flavor wheel" />
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="talk-slide">
            <p class="talk-kicker">17 · Close</p>
            <h1 class="talk-title">Climb, then brew</h1>
            <p class="talk-lede">Coast, then the lower selva, then Jaén and San Ignacio, then La Convención and Sandia. Height slows the cherry. The seed gets denser. That is the talk.</p>
            <p class="talk-copy">Questions — then we taste which mountain is in the cup.</p>
            <p class="talk-hint"><a href="/Resources">Back to Resources</a> · <a href="/Experiences">Experiences</a></p>
        </section>
    </main>

